<p><strong>FAA Remote Pilot Part 107 Exam Study Guide</strong></p>
<p>This chapter develops the operational knowledge needed to safely plan, conduct, and manage small unmanned aircraft system (sUAS) flights. It follows the Operations lesson material and emphasizes practical application, crew coordination, emergency planning, aeronautical decision making, human factors, and maintenance.</p>
<h2 id="chapter-objectives"><strong>Chapter Objectives</strong></h2>
<ul>
<li>Explain the remote pilot in command’s responsibility for determining that an sUAS is in a condition for safe operation.</li>
<li>Use a systematic preflight process to inspect the aircraft, control station, payload, batteries, communications links, and supporting equipment.</li>
<li>Interpret common airport traffic-pattern calls and basic aviation radio phraseology.</li>
<li>Explain how radio-frequency interference can affect UAS command, telemetry, and video links.</li>
<li>Describe appropriate planning and responses for lost link, flyaways, loss of GPS/GNSS, flight termination, and lithium-battery hazards.</li>
<li>Apply aeronautical decision making (ADM), crew resource management (CRM), and situational awareness to UAS operations.</li>
<li>Recognize hazardous attitudes and common physiological factors that can degrade pilot performance.</li>
<li>Explain basic maintenance and inspection practices and organize a safe training flight.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="4-1-the-remote-pic-and-safe-operation"><strong>4.1 The Remote PIC and Safe Operation</strong></h2>
<p>The remote pilot in command (remote PIC) is responsible for determining whether the aircraft is in a condition for safe flight. A successful flight on the previous mission is not proof that the system is safe for the next one. Transportation, a hard landing, a payload change, a repair, a firmware update, or a battery change can introduce new hazards.</p>
<p>Preflight inspection should therefore be thorough, systematic, and performed before each operation. A checklist helps the pilot perform the same critical checks every time and reduces the chance that familiarity or time pressure will cause a step to be skipped.</p>
<p>When a component is damaged or questionable, the conservative response is to remove the aircraft from service until the problem is corrected. Repairs and replacement of components should follow the manufacturer’s recommendations whenever they are available.</p>
<p class="textbook-callout">KEY POINT: The remote PIC makes the final determination that the entire UAS—not merely the airframe—is ready for safe operation.</p>
<h3 id="a-systematic-preflight-inspection"><strong>A Systematic Preflight Inspection</strong></h3>
<ul>
<li>Visually inspect the airframe and system components for cracks, looseness, bending, damage, or anything out of place.</li>
<li>For fixed-wing aircraft, inspect wings, fuselage, hinges, control linkages, control surfaces, propeller, motor, and attachment points.</li>
<li>Verify the correct battery or energy source, adequate charge for the mission and reserve, secure installation, and no swelling or physical damage.</li>
<li>Secure cameras, sensors, and other payload equipment. Payload movement can change center of gravity or allow equipment to separate in flight.</li>
<li>Check required registration markings and identification.</li>
<li>Inspect avionics, wiring, antennas, the controller/ground station, and communications links.</li>
<li>Perform sensor or compass calibration when required by the manufacturer or operating conditions.</li>
<li>Perform functional checks. For fixed-wing UAS, verify that ailerons, elevator, and rudder move freely and in the correct direction.</li>
<li>Confirm propulsion, GPS/GNSS, telemetry, and other mission-critical systems operate as expected.</li>
</ul>
<p class="textbook-callout">EXAM TIP: Think of the preflight as an inspection of the complete system: aircraft, power source, payload, controller, communications, navigation systems, crew, and operating environment.</p>
<h2 id="4-2-communication-and-crew-coordination"><strong>4.2 Communication and Crew Coordination</strong></h2>
<p>Good communication is a core element of crew resource management. The remote PIC, visual observer (VO), and other crew members should understand their roles before flight and should have a clear method for communicating hazards, traffic, aircraft status, and changes to the mission.</p>
<p>Crew members should continuously maintain situational awareness by scanning for crewed aircraft, people, obstacles, and other collision hazards. A visual observer can assist the remote PIC, but useful communication must be specific. A call such as “Traffic, two o’clock, low, moving left to right” provides far more information than simply shouting “airplane.”</p>
<p>Before flight, the crew should agree on important words such as abort, land, hold, traffic, lost link, and the phrases used to transfer control. Clear terminology reduces ambiguity during high-workload situations.</p>
