<h3 id="understanding-airport-traffic-calls"><strong>Understanding Airport Traffic Calls</strong></h3>
<p>When operating near an airport, a remote pilot should understand how crewed aircraft move through the traffic pattern. A standard pattern includes departure/upwind, crosswind, downwind, base, and final. Most standard patterns use left turns, although right-hand patterns are used at some runways.</p>
<p><strong>“Entering left downwind Runway 18” — </strong>The aircraft is parallel to Runway 18 on the downwind leg in a left-hand pattern.</p>
<p><strong>“Turning left base Runway 36” — </strong>The aircraft is approaching the runway environment and will soon turn final.</p>
<p><strong>“Short final Runway 36” — </strong>The aircraft is very close to the runway and about to land.</p>
<p><strong>“Upwind Runway 36, departing the area northwest” — </strong>The aircraft is climbing away from the runway and intends to leave the airport area toward the northwest.</p>
<p><strong>“Departing Runway 18, remaining in the pattern” — </strong>The aircraft will continue flying the airport traffic pattern rather than leaving the area.</p>
<p>Distance from an airport alone does not define the collision risk. The remote PIC should consider runway orientation, the active traffic pattern, and where aircraft are likely to climb, descend, or turn. Monitoring the CTAF can improve situational awareness when appropriate, but radio monitoring never replaces visual scanning because some aircraft may not transmit or may not have a radio.</p>
