<h3 id="initial-radio-contact"><strong>Initial Radio Contact</strong></h3>
<p>An initial callup is the first transmission to a particular ATC facility or the first call after being transferred to another controller or Flight Service specialist. A useful memory aid is:</p>
<p><strong>WHO you are calling → WHO you are → WHAT you want</strong></p>
<p>For example: “Bozeman Tower, November One Two Three Alpha Bravo, request transition through Class Delta airspace.” The word “over” is generally unnecessary in routine VHF aviation communication.</p>
<p>A longer ATC exchange may include a request, an assigned transponder code (“squawk”), an instruction to IDENT, confirmation of radar contact, altitude instructions, and an altimeter setting. Even if a remote pilot does not routinely talk to ATC, understanding this language helps the pilot interpret nearby crewed-aircraft activity.</p>
<h2 id="4-3-suas-radio-frequencies-and-interference"><strong>4.3 sUAS Radio Frequencies and Interference</strong></h2>
<p>UAS commonly depend on radio-frequency links for command and control, telemetry, and video/data transmission. In the United States, the radio-frequency spectrum is regulated by the Federal Communications Commission (FCC). Two bands commonly used by consumer and commercial UAS are 2.4 GHz and 5.8 GHz.</p>
<p>These bands are also used by Wi-Fi and many other devices. In dense RF environments, interference may reduce range, degrade video, increase latency, or cause a command-and-control link to be lost. Buildings, terrain, trees, vehicles, and antenna orientation can also reduce signal quality.</p>
<p>Modern systems may use either 2.4 GHz or 5.8 GHz—or switch between them—for control, telemetry, and video. Pilots should therefore know the behavior of the specific UAS rather than memorizing a fixed rule that one band is always control and the other is always video.</p>
<p class="textbook-callout">KEY POINT: Know what your aircraft will do if the radio link is lost. Lost-link behavior should be configured and understood before takeoff.</p>
<h2 id="4-4-emergency-and-abnormal-procedures"><strong>4.4 Emergency and Abnormal Procedures</strong></h2>
<p>Emergency planning is most effective when it occurs before the emergency. The remote PIC should know how the aircraft is configured to respond to failures and should decide in advance what actions the crew will take.</p>
<h3 id="lost-link"><strong>Lost Link</strong></h3>
<p>Lost link is the loss of the command-and-control communications link between the control station and the aircraft. Depending on the system, the aircraft may return to home, hold or loiter, continue a programmed route, or land. The pilot should know the programmed behavior, return-home altitude, home point, expected endurance, and any hazards along the automated route. If the event could affect controlled airspace or create a hazard to other aircraft, appropriate ATC notification may be necessary.</p>
<h3 id="flight-termination"><strong>Flight Termination</strong></h3>
<p>Flight termination means intentionally ending the flight when continuing would create a greater hazard. Depending on the aircraft, this could mean an immediate landing or use of a dedicated termination function. Protecting people and other aircraft takes priority over saving the UAS.</p>
<h3 id="flyaways"><strong>Flyaways</strong></h3>
<p>A flyaway occurs when the crew loses the ability to control the UAS and it continues in an unintended manner. Lost link may contribute, but navigation failures, incorrect programming, sensor faults, and other malfunctions can also be involved. If a flyaway threatens controlled airspace or other aircraft, the crew should provide useful information as quickly as practical, such as last known position, altitude, direction of flight, and estimated remaining endurance.</p>
<h3 id="loss-of-gps-gnss"><strong>Loss of GPS/GNSS</strong></h3>
<p>Loss of GPS/GNSS does not automatically mean loss of aircraft control. Many UAS can still be manually controlled, but position hold, autonomous navigation, geospatial functions, and return-to-home may be degraded or unavailable. The remote PIC should understand the aircraft’s non-GPS flight mode and have a plan for safely recovering or landing the aircraft.</p>
<h3 id="lithium-battery-fires"><strong>Lithium Battery Fires</strong></h3>
<p>Lithium-ion and lithium-polymer batteries can enter thermal runaway when damaged, overheated, overcharged, or otherwise compromised. Affected cells can become extremely hot and may ignite neighboring cells. Batteries that are swollen, punctured, physically damaged, unusually hot, or otherwise questionable should be removed from service.</p>
<p class="textbook-callout">SCENARIO: Your UAS loses GPS but still responds normally to manual control. Do not assume a flyaway is inevitable. Stabilize the aircraft, determine which automated functions are unavailable, and use the predetermined recovery plan for that aircraft.</p>
<h2 id="4-5-aeronautical-decision-making"><strong>4.5 Aeronautical Decision Making</strong></h2>
<p>Aeronautical Decision Making (ADM) is a systematic approach to the mental process pilots use to determine the best course of action for a given set of circumstances. Good ADM is based on recognizing hazards, evaluating risk, and deliberately reducing risk before it develops into an accident.</p>
<div class="textbook-table" role="region" aria-label="4.5 Aeronautical Decision Making — table 3" tabindex="0"><table><caption>4.5 Aeronautical Decision Making — table 3</caption>
<thead>
<tr>
<th scope="col">Concept</th>
<th scope="col">Operational Meaning</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Situational Awareness</th>
<td>Understanding what is happening around the operation and anticipating what may happen next. Airspace, weather, terrain, obstacles, people, nearby aircraft, privacy concerns, and regulatory requirements all contribute.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Aeronautical Decision Making (ADM)</th>
<td>A systematic process for identifying hazards, assessing risk, comparing alternatives, and choosing a safe course of action.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Crew Resource Management (CRM)</th>
<td>Effective use of available people, equipment, information, checklists, and other resources before and during flight.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Safety Management System (SMS)</th>
<td>An organizational approach to managing safety through defined responsibilities, policies, procedures, risk-management processes, and continuous improvement.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>These ideas reinforce one another: situational awareness asks what is happening; ADM asks what should be done; CRM asks how people and resources can be used effectively; and SMS asks how safety can be built into the organization.</p>
<h3 id="the-five-hazardous-attitudes"><strong>The Five Hazardous Attitudes</strong></h3>
<div class="textbook-table" role="region" aria-label="The Five Hazardous Attitudes — table 4" tabindex="0"><table><caption>The Five Hazardous Attitudes — table 4</caption>
<thead>
<tr>
<th scope="col">Hazardous attitude</th>
<th scope="col">Typical thought</th>
<th scope="col">Antidote</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Anti-authority</th>
<td>“Don’t tell me.”</td>
<td>Follow the rules; they are usually right.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Impulsivity</th>
<td>“Do it quickly.”</td>
<td>Not so fast—think first.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Invulnerability</th>
<td>“It won’t happen to me.”</td>
<td>It could happen to me.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Macho</th>
<td>“I can do it.”</td>
<td>Taking chances is foolish.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Resignation</th>
<td>“What’s the use?”</td>
<td>I’m not helpless; I can make a difference.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>Hazardous attitudes can affect pilots at any experience level. Repeated success can even make an experienced pilot more willing to normalize risk. A useful self-check is: Am I making this decision because it is the safest option, or because I want the flight to happen?</p>
<h2 id="4-6-human-factors-and-physiology"><strong>4.6 Human Factors and Physiology</strong></h2>
<p>A remote pilot operates from the ground, but the same human limitations that affect crewed-aircraft pilots can degrade UAS performance. Judgment, situational awareness, reaction time, and decision making can all be affected by physical and mental condition.</p>
<p><strong>Hyperventilation: </strong>Rapid or deep breathing associated with stress, anxiety, or fear can reduce carbon dioxide in the blood and cause dizziness, tingling, lightheadedness, confusion, or fainting.</p>
<p><strong>Dehydration: </strong>Loss of body water can contribute to headaches, fatigue, cramps, sleepiness, dizziness, and reduced concentration.</p>
<p><strong>Heatstroke: </strong>Failure of the body’s temperature-regulation system. Confusion, altered behavior, collapse, or loss of consciousness are serious warning signs.</p>
<p><strong>Hypothermia: </strong>Excessively low body temperature. Symptoms may include shivering, clumsiness, slurred speech, confusion, low energy, skin discoloration, and loss of consciousness.</p>
<p><strong>Stress: </strong>Can result from weather, equipment problems, inexperienced crew, spectators, time pressure, or mission demands. Excessive stress can narrow attention and impair decisions.</p>
<p><strong>Fatigue: </strong>May be acute or chronic. Lack of sleep, long missions, repetitive tasks, stress, heat, cold, and sustained concentration can slow reactions and increase errors.</p>
<p><strong>Chronic stress: </strong>Long-term unresolved stress can degrade sleep, concentration, judgment, and overall performance.</p>
<p class="textbook-callout">KEY POINT: Preflight the pilot and crew as deliberately as you preflight the aircraft. Being present is not the same as being fit to fly.</p>
<h2 id="4-7-maintenance-and-inspection"><strong>4.7 Maintenance and Inspection</strong></h2>
<p>Proper maintenance extends the service life of an sUAS and helps ensure that it remains safe to operate. Manufacturer inspection intervals, maintenance instructions, component limitations, and replacement schedules should be followed when available.</p>
<p>For custom-built or experimental systems without a complete manufacturer maintenance program, the operator should develop documented procedures appropriate to the aircraft. Useful records may include flight hours, number of flights, battery cycles, motor hours, propeller condition, servo operation, control linkages, structural inspections, repairs, firmware changes, and payload changes.</p>
<p>When different components have different inspection or replacement intervals, using the most restrictive applicable limitation provides a conservative margin. After repairs or maintenance, perform appropriate functional checks before returning the aircraft to normal operation.</p>
<p>For a fixed-wing UAS, post-maintenance checks should include structural integrity, propulsion, communications, navigation, and correct control-surface movement and direction.</p>
<h2 id="4-8-organizing-a-safe-training-flight"><strong>4.8 Organizing a Safe Training Flight</strong></h2>
<p>A training flight should be a structured learning environment rather than an improvised flight. The objective is to give the student room to learn while controlling risk.</p>
<ol>
<li>Check weather and use conservative limits appropriate to the student’s skill level.</li>
<li>Establish a clearly defined flight zone.</li>
<li>Establish an observer/spectator area and a pilot/instructor area.</li>
<li>Preflight the aircraft and supporting equipment.</li>
<li>Verify controls are free and correct.</li>
<li>Verify the battery is secure and has adequate charge.</li>
<li>If using a buddy-box or dual-control system, test transfer and instructor override before launch.</li>
<li>Visually inspect the aircraft and check the surrounding airspace.</li>
<li>Preflight the student: alert, focused, distractions controlled, and aware of the plan.</li>
<li>Brief the sequence of the flight and what the student should do if orientation or control is lost.</li>
<li>Use positive transfer-of-control language: “You have control.” / “I have control.”</li>
<li>Debrief after the flight.</li>
</ol>
<p>A useful sequence is: Brief → Preflight → Launch → Establish a safe flight condition → Transfer control → Practice → Recover → Debrief.</p>
<h2 id="chapter-4-summary"><strong>Chapter 4 Summary</strong></h2>
<ul>
<li>The remote PIC is responsible for determining that the UAS is in a condition for safe operation.</li>
<li>Preflight inspections should be systematic and include the entire UAS, not just the aircraft.</li>
<li>Clear crew communication and situational awareness are essential to safe operations.</li>
<li>Understanding airport traffic patterns and common radio phraseology helps remote pilots anticipate crewed-aircraft activity.</li>
<li>2.4 GHz and 5.8 GHz are common UAS frequency bands, but their exact use depends on the system.</li>
<li>Lost link, flyaways, loss of GPS/GNSS, flight termination, and lithium-battery hazards should be planned for before launch.</li>
<li>ADM, CRM, situational awareness, and SMS provide complementary approaches to managing aviation risk.</li>
<li>The five hazardous attitudes are anti-authority, impulsivity, invulnerability, macho, and resignation.</li>
<li>Physiology, stress, fatigue, dehydration, heat, and cold can degrade remote-pilot performance.</li>
<li>Maintenance, records, functional checks, and structured training procedures help create repeatable safe operations.</li>
</ul>
